WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.367
It's two a.m. at a hackathon. Kabir's chatbot

2
00:00:03.437 --> 00:00:06.072
finally works, and then his free API credits

3
00:00:06.118 --> 00:00:07.198
run out.

4
00:00:07.548 --> 00:00:09.487
He doesn't need more credits. He needs a

5
00:00:09.557 --> 00:00:11.867
model that runs on his own laptop.

6
00:00:12.217 --> 00:00:15.038
That's what Ollama does. It's a small server:

7
00:00:15.178 --> 00:00:17.790
it downloads open model weights, like Llama or

8
00:00:17.859 --> 00:00:20.390
Qwen, and loads them into memory.

9
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.538
Then it serves an API on localhost, right

10
00:00:23.573 --> 00:00:24.920
on his machine.

11
00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.708
One command pulls a model. One more, and

12
00:00:27.755 --> 00:00:29.496
he's chatting in the terminal.

13
00:00:29.846 --> 00:00:32.133
And here's the trick. Ollama speaks the same

14
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.629
format as the OpenAI API.

15
00:00:34.979 --> 00:00:37.289
So Kabir changes the base URL and the

16
00:00:37.324 --> 00:00:39.286
model name, and the rest of his code

17
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:41.202
stays exactly the same.

18
00:00:41.552 --> 00:00:43.851
Build and debug on the free local model,

19
00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:46.126
then flip one setting to a hosted model

20
00:00:46.184 --> 00:00:47.403
for the demo.

21
00:00:47.753 --> 00:00:50.388
The catch is memory. Every weight must fit

22
00:00:50.447 --> 00:00:53.651
in RAM, so local models are quantised: about

23
00:00:53.721 --> 00:00:56.437
four bits per weight instead of sixteen.

24
00:00:56.787 --> 00:00:59.353
On an eight gigabyte laptop, a three billion

25
00:00:59.411 --> 00:01:03.103
parameter model fits. Bigger models need more RAM.

26
00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:06.309
What do you get? No bill, no internet,

27
00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:08.840
and nothing you type leaves the laptop.

28
00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.361
What do you give up? It's smaller and

29
00:01:11.431 --> 00:01:13.788
slower than a hosted model, so test both

30
00:01:13.834 --> 00:01:15.645
on your own questions.

31
00:01:15.995 --> 00:01:19.339
Remember it like this: same SDK, different base

32
00:01:19.385 --> 00:01:20.453
URL.

33
00:01:20.803 --> 00:01:23.822
Next lesson: prompting, RAG, or fine-tuning?
